B.push(P(`**Bearings.** On the rolling bearings the model failed completely: none of the ${FG.n} first warnings fell in the window; ${FG.early} warnings were too early and ${FG.missed} bearings were never warned. The competitors did no better (age-only ${FA.IN} of ${FA.n}, MLP with Kalman filter ${FM.IN}), so no hypothesis was supported. The pre-specified official challenge split (6 training, 11 test bearings) gave the same picture (Supplementary Section S7). A diagnosis made after the run, with an ad hoc criterion, explains part of this: in ${N.bearing_onset.n_le6} of the ${FG.n} bearings the vibration level rose to and stayed above 1.5 times its early-life level only in the last six blocks (about seven minutes) before failure, whereas a correct warning at *H* = 20 needs a signal about 20 blocks before failure (Supplementary Table S1). It does not explain everything: the model also missed or warned too early on bearings with long degradation, and raised early false alarms on several abruptly failing ones. Small numbers (13–14 training bearings per fold) and three different operating conditions are equally plausible causes. The pre-specified theory-guided shift (STW-T) was equally unsuccessful (${f1(N.stwt.Bearing['20'].stwt)}%; 0 against 0 discordant bearings, *p* = 1.000), so its non-inferiority criterion was met only trivially and the test was uninformative.`));
