B.push(P(`Fig. 8 checks Proposition 1 on every combination of dataset and warning level. For each, we took the test warnings, recovered the unshifted error ε = *e* − *s*, and computed its median *m* and its centred concentration Pr(|ε − *m*| ≤ 7.5); missed units count as failures. On the seven datasets other than the bearings, the realised in-window rate stayed close to the concentration (mean absolute difference ${f1(ths.mean_abs_gap_nb)} percentage points, largest ${f1(ths.max_abs_gap_nb)}), sometimes above it, because the empirical errors are not exactly symmetric and assumption A1 holds only approximately; the concentration is therefore a reference value rather than a strict bound, and since both quantities come from the same warnings their agreement is partly built in. The informative contrast is between datasets: at *H* = 20 the concentration was ${f1(tB.centred_bound_pct)}% on the batteries, ${f1(tP.centred_bound_pct)}% on PHM08, ${f1(t4.centred_bound_pct)}% on FD004, ${f1(tN.centred_bound_pct)}% on N-CMAPSS, the same ordering as the realised rates; on the bearings, where 10 of 17 units were never warned, the concentration is not meaningful.`));
B.push(P(`If the datasets differ in concentration rather than in how well the shift is set, a better shift rule should not help, and it did not: choosing the shift so that the median out-of-fold error equals the window centre (STW-T) changed the rate at *H* = 20 by between ${f1(Math.min(...dT))} and ${f1(Math.max(...dT))} percentage points on the seven datasets seen before it was written (Supplementary Section S10). Section 6.3 adds the practical reading: since the reported estimate hardly varies at the alarm, the concentration of ε is essentially the concentration of the alarm time relative to failure. The rate also grew with the width of the window on every dataset (Supplementary Section S8).`));
