must(WDS.every(d => wp(d, -10, 2) <= wp(d, -13, 2) + 0.05 && wp(d, -13, 2) <= wp(d, -20, 5) + 0.05 && wp(d, -20, 5) <= wp(d, -30, 10) + 0.05), 'in-window rate increases with window width on every dataset');

// ================================================================ 7 Discussion
B.push(H1('7. Discussion'));
B.push(H2('7.1. Why self-timing works, and what it does not do'));
B.push(P(`The warning model is not a better long-range predictor; its regressor was designed for, and evaluated at, the end of life only. What it does is time an alarm (Section 6.3): the score rewards an alarm raised roughly 13 to 28 cycles before failure at *H* = 20, and the regressor earns its place as a health indicator calibrated in cycles to failure, which a classical linear health indicator with a tuned threshold could not replace. The ablation agrees: removing the window-centring shift, which moves the alarm, cost the most, followed by removing the target cap and reducing the sensor set, whereas smoothing, debouncing and the exact cap value mattered little, and the oracle-timed control shows that letting the model choose its own moment did not cost accuracy. The same reading explains Section 6.6: the window tolerates 13 cycles of early error but only 2 of late error, so tuning for it builds a margin into the alarm, and choosing only the warning level by cost gave the lowest regret, whereas minimising the training cost directly was fragile when failures were expensive. The regressor itself is exchangeable (Section 6.4): with our inputs, a hybrid Transformer matched gradient boosting (${f1(N.dl['tf15|20'].pct)}% against ${f1(h2['ours|20'].pct)}%) at about ${Math.round(N.compute.dl_s.slice().sort((x, y) => x - y)[2] / 60)} minutes of training per fold against about ${Math.round(Math.max(...N.compute.stw_s))} seconds, so gradient boosting remains our default for its speed and auditability. No claim about the PHM08 challenge leaderboard follows, because the task and data split differ. The model does not provide long-range planning information, for which fixed-time predictions and their uncertainty remain the relevant tools (Kamariotis et al., 2024; Mitici et al., 2023); a planning model sets the maintenance slot, and the warning model triggers the final removal.`));
B.push(H2('7.2. When the method works: lessons from the three confirmations'));
B.push(P(`The confirmations are the most informative results of this study, because their protocols were fixed before any warning result on those data was seen, and together they delimit the method. It works when the approach of failure becomes visible, consistently across units, some time before the warning level: battery capacity declines slowly and then drops at a knee, and the error at the warning was more concentrated than on any engine dataset (interquartile range ${f1(tB.eps_iqr)} blocks). It degrades when that signal is noisy relative to the remaining time, as on N-CMAPSS, where short lives place a 20-cycle warning at about three quarters of life and only ${f1(tN.centred_bound_pct)}% of the errors lay within ±7.5 cycles of their median; neither more channels nor a theory-guided shift helped. It failed completely on the bearings, where most failures came within minutes of a sustained rise in vibration, although that post hoc diagnosis does not explain every bearing and the small number of units may matter as much. A shift between test campaigns also costs accuracy, as the battery leave-one-batch-out analysis showed (${f1(BL.pct)}%). Proposition 1 gives a practical check before deployment: estimate the concentration of the out-of-fold error at the intended warning level on the fleet's own run-to-failure history; if it is low, no choice of shift will rescue the rate, and either the warning level, the window or the sensing must change.`));
B.push(H2('7.3. Limitations'));
B.push(...LI([
  `• **No field fleet.** Six of the eight datasets are simulations, and the battery and bearing data are laboratory measurements. The confirmations range from ${f1(BW.pct)}% to ${f1(FG.pct)}%, so the attainable rate depends on the system and must be re-estimated for each application.`,
  '• **Development on PHM08.** The model and the grids were fixed while looking at PHM08 results, and the primary level was designated after the C-MAPSS results were known, so these estimates may be optimistic; only the three confirmations were pre-specified, and their hashes were not deposited with a registry.',
  '• **Post hoc secondary analyses.** The constant-estimate control, the health-indicator alarm, the cost-tuned policies, the ablation, the oracle-timed control, the networks with equal inputs, the other windows, the cost analysis, the 14-channel N-CMAPSS run, the check of Proposition 1, STW-T on the seen datasets, the thirty random 50-engine tests and the bearing diagnosis were added after the main results were known. They describe the method; they are not confirmations.',
  COMPETITOR_LIMITATION(),
  '• **Cost-tuned comparison.** The cost-tuned policies choose among about two thousand settings, whereas the window-tuned policy chooses only its warning level by cost after the window tuning, so part of the fragility of direct cost tuning reflects selection over a larger grid on limited training units; a regularised or probabilistic cost tuning (Kamariotis et al., 2024) might close the gap. The health-indicator alarm is one classical baseline, not the strongest possible.',
  '• **A simple cost model.** The renewal–reward model assumes a fixed lead time, independent units and replacement as good as new, and the cost ratios are illustrative rather than measured; it ignores spare-part inventory, downtime and the grouping of maintenance across units (de Pater et al., 2022; Mitici et al., 2023).']));

// ================================================================ 8 Conclusion
// ================================================================ 8 Future work (RESS version)
must(N.bearing_onset.n_le6 === 11 && cv.FD004['20'].gross_early === 6 && cv.FD004['20'].early === 26, 'future-work facts: bearing onset 11 of 17, FD004 6 of 26 gross early warnings');
B.push(H1('8. Future work: test and maintenance policies, and automatic fault detection and diagnosis'));
B.push(P(`The warning model was developed and evaluated as a trigger for the replacement of single units. Its two ingredients, an alarm rule tuned on run-to-failure histories and a leakage-safe protocol for evaluating that rule, are not tied to this use. Two directions within the scope of this journal follow directly, and the present results indicate both why the model is suited to them and what remains to be shown (Fig. 9).`));
B.push(...figure(__dirname + '/img/Fig9_future_work.png', 'Planned uses of the self-timed warning model in test and maintenance policies (left) and in automatic fault detection and diagnosis (right). Solid boxes: evaluated in this paper; dashed boxes: future work.'));
B.push(H2('8.1. Test and maintenance policies'));
B.push(P(`Condition-based replacement with a lead time is the first application, and Section 6.6 gives its long-run cost against age replacement and perfect foresight. Three extensions are natural. First, logistics: an alarm can order the spare part, so the warning level should be chosen jointly with the order lead time and the cost of a failure; Section 6.6 already chooses the level by cost for a given lead time, and a closed-form or dynamic-programming rule for this choice would make the policy transparent. Second, fleets: alarms of several units can be grouped into shared maintenance visits, as in alarm-based scheduling for aircraft engines (de Pater et al., 2022) and dynamic maintenance of multiple components (Mitici et al., 2023). How much slack such grouping has is set by the spread of the alarm time relative to failure, the quantity that Proposition 1 and Section 6.3 identify as the limit of the in-window rate. Third, imperfect repair, downtime and spare-part stocks, which our renewal–reward model leaves out.`));
B.push(P(`The model can also set **test and inspection policies**, a long-standing topic of maintenance optimisation (de Jonge & Scarf, 2020), for units that are not monitored continuously, such as standby equipment that is proof-tested or structures that are inspected at intervals. There the alarm would trigger an additional test rather than a replacement, while a baseline test interval covers the remaining risk. Two pieces are missing: an encoder for sparse and irregular observations, since the present encoder assumes one observation per cycle, and a cost model with test costs, test-induced downtime and the chance that a test reveals a dormant failure. The asymmetric window has a direct reading in this setting, its early limit being the margin accepted between test and failure and its late limit the risk of testing too late, and Proposition 1 gives a check before such a policy is fielded: if the alarm time is not concentrated at the intended level, no threshold will make on-demand testing reliable, and the baseline interval must carry the risk. Because alarms tuned on one test campaign lost accuracy on another (battery leave-one-batch-out: ${f1(BL.pct)}%), such policies should also include a fallback, for example age-based replacement or the baseline test interval for units outside the conditions seen in training.`));
B.push(H2('8.2. Automatic fault detection and diagnosis'));
B.push(P(`The trigger of Section 4.4 is a sequential decision rule, a debounced threshold crossing on a smoothed statistic, of the same kind as the control charts and run rules used for data-driven fault detection (Venkatasubramanian et al., 2003). Section 6.3 showed that, under the asymmetric window, the score rewards the timing of the alarm, which is also what a fault detector trades off: detection delay against false alarms. A first study should therefore compare the warning model with established detectors, such as cumulative-sum (Page, 1954) and exponentially weighted moving-average charts (Roberts, 1959) and Hotelling's T² statistic on the regime-normalised sensors, under both the window and the usual detection metrics (detection delay, false-alarm rate and missed detections), with the same nested, leakage-safe protocol. Gross false alarms, more than 30 cycles early, were rare in our results except on FD004 (${cv.FD004['20'].gross_early} of its ${cv.FD004['20'].early} too-early warnings at *H* = 20), which gives a reference for that comparison.`));
B.push(P(`Diagnosis is the second step. FD003 and FD004 contain two fault modes, and FD004 had the lowest in-window rate of the C-MAPSS datasets. A diagnostic extension would estimate the fault mode together with the remaining life and issue mode-specific alarms, with the warning level and window set per mode. Because C-MAPSS does not label the fault mode of each engine, this requires data with labelled modes, such as N-CMAPSS, whose subsets simulate different failure modes. Abrupt faults are the third topic. On the bearings, the vibration level rose to and stayed above 1.5 times its early-life level only in the last six blocks before failure in ${N.bearing_onset.n_le6} of ${N.bearing_data.n} units, so no alarm timed from these features could give 20 blocks of notice. A two-stage design, in which a fast detector on high-frequency vibration features declares the onset of a defect and the warning model then times the replacement, is a plausible remedy that we have not tested. Finally, the regime-normalised, baseline-removed signals behave like residuals and could be used to flag sensor faults before they corrupt the alarm; the robustness of the warning model to sensor faults has not been examined.`));
B.push(P(`For both directions, the evaluation practice of this paper carries over unchanged: nested selection by unit, controls that separate what a score measures from what it is meant to measure, and protocols hashed before confirmation data are analysed.`));

B.push(H1('9. Conclusion'));
B.push(P(`Requiring a remaining-life estimate to lie within −13 to +2 cycles is not achievable at fixed times far from failure: on PHM08, eleven models reached at most ${f1(halfMax)}% at half of life. Letting the model decide when to speak changes the picture. A horizon-focused gradient-boosted regressor with a window-centred, debounced trigger, tuned by nested cross-validation, placed ${f1(P08.pct)}% of PHM08 first warnings and ${f1(pool['20'].pct)}% of ${pool['20'].n} simulated first warnings inside the window at a warning level of 20 cycles${CONCL_COMPARE()}. What this score measures is the alarm time: a constant estimate at the same times scored ${f1(R2.pooled['20'].STW_const.pct)}%, whereas a classical health-indicator alarm scored ${f1(R2.pooled['20'].HI_threshold.pct)}%. Priced in a renewal–reward model, window-tuned alarms were cheaper than optimal age replacement in ${engAge} of 96 settings on the engines and batteries and had lower regret than alarms tuned directly to the cost, so the asymmetric window serves as a robust surrogate for the maintenance cost. Three pre-specified confirmations show where this holds: the method succeeded on measured battery degradation (${f1(BW.pct)}%, all four hypotheses supported), fell short on N-CMAPSS (${f1(NW.pct)}%), where the alarm time is less concentrated and the alarms were no cheaper than age replacement, and failed on rolling bearings (${f1(N.bearing_detail['cv|warning|20'].pct)}%). The approach is simple, fast and easy to audit; before it is relied on, the concentration of its alarm time at the intended warning level should be estimated on the fleet's own run-to-failure history. Section 8 outlines how the same alarm can set test and maintenance policies and serve automatic fault detection and diagnosis.`));

